    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d192">

                <pb facs="0310" n="198" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg198"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/3795: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1919-02-03T13:15:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1919-02-02T13:00:00+04:53"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received February 3, 1919, 1:15
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Following from [Harris at] Omsk.</p>
                <p>“82. February 2, 1 p.m. Foreign Office Omsk informs me that on January 28
                    Japanese Minister of War Tanaka again<anchor n="3" xml:id="d192-f1"/> offered
                    services his Government at furnishing all necessary arms and ammunition to
                    Siberian Army. Japanese Government declared its intention to form new divisions
                    in east, together with Russians, to fight against Bolsheviki. Kolchak government
                    is declining this offer, stating that whatever action taken should be done
                    jointly and harmoniously by all Allies. Japanese Government is sending two
                    generals <pb facs="0311" n="199" xml:id="pg199"/> and one Admiral to Omsk to
                    confer with Kolchak. Foreign Office also states that it has received telegram
                    from General Yudenich, commanding Esthonian and other [omission]. He states that
                    he could take Petrograd but cannot provision the city. He is asking Allies to
                    occupy Russian Baltic ports with a view of affording relief to city after its
                    capture. Harris.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d192-f1" n="3" xml:id="d192-f1-content">
                <p>The earlier offer referred to may be that reported in a despatch from General
                    Graves, Dec. 13, 1918, <ref target="#pg466">p. 466</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
